WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.323
It's the annual eating of
the herring in Finland.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.690
For one week in October,

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.410
people come here to downtown Helsinki

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.750
to gorge on the freshest of fish,

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.963
and other delicious, smoky treats.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:34.000
They jockey for position,
prepare Instagram posts,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.503
and enjoy some Tolkien-inspired folk.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.923
As you may have heard,
life is good for the Finns.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.573
Their kids are smarter than yours.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.143
Their health system is
healthier than yours.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.393
And my God, their parks
are parkier than yours.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.750
These Finns also eat really well.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.450
On a square plate, there's some molt fed,

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:24.420
lightly smoked pork and
wild garlic emulsion on top.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:28.133
On our round plate, beetroot marshmallow.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.600
Here I am at your run-of-the-mill
fast, casual joint.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:37.853
Popping some bubbly, and gobbling
up a ham and cheese roll.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:42.713
Followed by a NASA-inspired
marshmallow made out of beets.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:44.523
That is good.

00:01:45.392 --> 00:01:46.225
That's crazy.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.430
But the future of food for Finland

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.533
and the rest of the world may be here,

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.260
where protein is grown in a vat,

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.053
overseen by the Finnish
version of Walter White.

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:16.360
This is Solar Foods, with
its headquarters in Espoo

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.113
on the outskirts of Helsinki.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.140
We make food out of thin air

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:26.170
using just electricity,
water, CO2, and some minerals

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.010
as the main ingredients.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.910
Have you ever seen Breaking Bad?

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.450
Yeah.
Yeah?

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.510
Is there anything else we should know

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:34.603
that happens in here?

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.470
This is pretty legal.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:39.303
Okay .

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:46.530
Until the pivot to full-blown
meth lab is needed,

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.170
Solar Foods is focusing on making protein

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.330
and other nutrients with as little water

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.253
and other resources as possible.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:59.240
No cows, no soy fields, just
bacteria being fed in a tub

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:03.463
by nature, and harvested by
lab coat-wearing laborers.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.470
In a sense what we are
making is an ingredient

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:08.870
for different food products.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:13.490
So tofu, yogurts, breakfast bars,

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:16.133
or any meal that one could think of.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:20.890
Solar Foods grabs CO2 from
the fresh, Finnish air

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:23.350
and hydrogen, produced via solar panels,

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.250
and uses this fuel to feed a microorganism

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:29.440
discovered by scientists in Finnish soil.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.120
The bacteria is then
fed a cocktail of water

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.010
and a secret blend of elements

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:37.060
such as iron, sulfur, and calcium.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.440
This slurry of goodness is then dried

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.780
to produce a powder that's 65% protein

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.823
with a few fatty acids and
carbs making up the rest.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.840
This is the end product,
this yellowish powder.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.480
Protein powder.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.300
And then this is just
the liquid form of that.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.850
And how long does the drying process take?

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.573
It takes about a day to dry one kilo.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.010
Solar Foods calls this
stuff Solein powder.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.250
Is it vetted by the FDA yet?

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:10.440
No.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.530
Is it safe for consumption?

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.100
I'm told yes.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.080
And since I'm the guinea
pig for a technology show,

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.100
my gut is your gut.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.350
This is my personal vegan pancake recipe.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:23.920
It's like the regular ingredients

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.180
for pancakes, pretty much, except--

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.220
Yeah, yeah, except we're replacing

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:29.333
eggs with Solein powder.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:32.303
That's the magic moment.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.810
All right, here goes our batter.

00:04:39.031 --> 00:04:40.700
Oh yeah, you're a pro.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.290
Do you wanna try it first?
I'll try it.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.513
This is like the pancake of the future.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:51.463
I've never eaten pancakes
grown in a laboratory before.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:57.740
It's a delicious pancake.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.983
Those dudes may cook
a mean protein powder,

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.450
but the true star of the
Finnish healthier food movement

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.190
is this woman, Maija Itkonen,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:14.063
the co-founder and CEO of Gold &amp; Green.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.220
Gold &amp; Green makes a vegetable-based

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.363
meat replacement called Pulled Oats.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:24.250
Everyone I met in Finland eats it.

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.803
It's in the grocery stores
in a variety of flavors,

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.090
and you can even get it at Taco Bell

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:31.633
inside the vegan burrito.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:33.940
It's a thing.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.653
A brown, chunky thing.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:40.460
Maija and I met in a
former Nokia cable factory,

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.840
where else, to try out some Pulled Oats.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.784
You're not trying to mimic
meat necessarily at all, right?

00:05:46.784 --> 00:05:47.853
You're just trying to make something

00:05:47.853 --> 00:05:50.600
that's sort of delicious,
that's made from oats.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:51.530
That's exactly true,

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:54.720
and it might be that the final
application is mimicking,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:55.630
because when you make a burger,

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:58.000
it needs to be something
that feels like a burger.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.000
But what actually doesn't mimic

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.410
is the ingredient list.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.390
The ingredient list, we
actually want to make sure

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.020
that you have few things

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.520
and they're really healthy
and really clean label,

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:09.120
and everything is based on that.

00:06:10.786 --> 00:06:12.040
What are the big advantages of oats?

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:15.553
Because it's not meat,
or, I mean, why oats?

00:06:15.553 --> 00:06:16.386
It's nutritional.

00:06:16.386 --> 00:06:17.860
It's like, marvelous.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.730
It has so good health
benefits for your heart

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.900
and digestion and blood sugar.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:24.350
It's like medicine, you know.

00:06:25.193 --> 00:06:29.060
Okay.
Thanks so much.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:29.960
It's like a curry?

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.440
It's delicious, I mean I would eat this

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:37.440
any day of the week.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.100
So, when we got started,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.040
we didn't even plan to sell in Finland.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:43.640
This was like two years ago or?

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:45.550
This was 2016.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:48.430
But we thought, let's just aim to test it

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:50.490
in couple of grocery stores.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.880
Then it was so funny,
because what happened

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:55.490
was that people got entirely crazy.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.627
All the newspapers started to call us

00:06:57.627 --> 00:06:59.670
and all the buyers started to call us,

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:02.840
so it was like, okay we're
going to sell this in Finland.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:06.623
And actually the market grew
like 700% during that year.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:15.380
Unlike just about every
other company in Finland,

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:16.670
Gold &amp; Green's headquarters

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:18.853
are not in a former Nokia building.

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:22.143
They're in a former bra factory.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.323
And its elevator is real and spectacular.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:30.800
This is where Pulled Oats come to life.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.180
So these are the basic ingredients?

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.990
Yeah, so actually this is oat flour.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:39.580
Then we have a fava bean flour.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.260
Then we also have a
yellow pea, pea protein,

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:43.470
but that's about it.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.500
Okay, and then the magic,

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:48.860
the secret is how you
blend this all together?

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:49.880
Absolutely, yes.

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:54.819
So we first make a dry material
base of these ingredients.

00:07:54.819 --> 00:07:56.990
Okay.
Then we start moisturizing

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:59.203
and baking it, and it
becomes something like this.

00:07:59.203 --> 00:08:00.682
Like this, okay.

00:08:00.682 --> 00:08:02.239
So this, so all this stuff--
You can taste it,

00:08:02.239 --> 00:08:03.072
yes absolutely.
This goes through

00:08:03.072 --> 00:08:07.330
like a machine, and the
machine and the process,

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.440
that's all top secret.
Top Secret.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:10.740
Nobody gets to see that.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.280
But I am allowed to see this.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:18.483
A brand new venture for Gold &amp; Green.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:24.143
And it's sizzling, salty, and porkish.

00:08:24.143 --> 00:08:27.820
It's something that can be
made to replace like chicken

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:29.146
or even like--
I know what we're doing,

00:08:29.146 --> 00:08:30.245
we're doing bacon.

00:08:30.245 --> 00:08:32.412
And this is not a bacon.

00:08:33.875 --> 00:08:36.300
Maija refuses to call it bacon,

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:38.890
but it's pretty much bacon.

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:40.373
And it might even be good.

00:08:41.968 --> 00:08:42.850
I mean, that's delicious.

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:46.160
It's got all the nice, salty,
fatty kind of feel to it.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:46.993
Yeah, yeah.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:49.823
I like this a lot.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.503
And now, thanks to a brave
volunteer from Gold &amp; Green,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.950
a better living through Finland Interlude

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.590
that will come as quite the shock

00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:07.143
to my American friends.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:11.263
I present to you a baby,

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.373
a mother, and a baby box.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:19.050
This is something that
the government sends

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:21.190
to every mother.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:23.100
Yes, or you can choose.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.150
You maybe don't want this,
and you can take the money.

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:28.670
So it's 170 euros.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:31.600
The box, is this meant to be a crib?

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:36.200
Yes, it is like a safe
and comfy place to sleep,

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:41.099
but also in this box there is 63 items.

00:09:41.099 --> 00:09:42.910
Okay, you get a book.
There is like,

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:44.123
the first baby book.

00:09:46.854 --> 00:09:49.254
You get all these clothes?
All the clothes.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:55.220
In Finland, it is quite common
that you leave the child

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:57.900
outside to take a nap.
Okay.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.590
So that's why we need a lot of clothes,

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:02.760
so the baby is warm.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.480
They sleep outside?
Yes.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:06.743
That's all seasons, or
just when it's warm?

00:10:06.743 --> 00:10:08.090
Also the winter time.

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:09.800
Why do they sleep outside?

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:12.360
I think it's good for you.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:15.003
How much maternity leave
do you get in Finland?

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:16.713
Nine months.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.880
In the box, there is
also the hygiene things.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:26.833
It is for the baby and also for adults.

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:30.498
You get like a thermometer,

00:10:30.498 --> 00:10:31.560
a brush.
There is the thermometer.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.940
Oh I think, are these condoms?

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:33.773
Yes, yeah.

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:37.753
Man, you guys think of everything.

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:40.510
I think I know what that is .

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:43.610
One day the America dream

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:46.697
will also include
state-provided lube, right?

00:10:47.821 --> 00:10:48.654
Right?

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:58.060
By now, you're probably thinking,

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:01.220
these Finns are godless, vegan socialists

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:04.370
who have an unnatural
amount of concern for nature

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:05.713
and general well-being.

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:09.093
This is probably even true.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:12.890
They also have a thing for relaxing

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:14.930
in the glorious outdoors,

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:18.103
like this open-air oasis
on Helsinki's waterfront.

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:23.463
I give to you, the Allas Pools.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:38.553
Steamed to perfection.

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:45.493
And refreshed and frozen
by the Baltic Sea.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.650
I return to the main streets of Helsinki

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:53.000
to find out the other ways

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.693
in which the Finns are
better than the rest of us.

00:11:58.010 --> 00:12:01.920
Which brought me here to
a startup and Suvi Haimi.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:04.580
She is the co-founder and CEO of Sulapac.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:08.233
And they wanna replace plastic with this.

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:13.350
What is important in our material,

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:15.230
it's microplastic free.

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:19.020
Eventually, plastic degrades
into microplastic particles,

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:20.990
and they stay hundreds of years

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:23.270
or even permanently in the nature,

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:26.750
but we have created almost
all the benefits of plastic

00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:28.450
without the plastic waste problem.

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:33.700
The big idea here is to take waste wood,

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:35.690
mash it up with some plant matter,

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:37.490
and create a substance that can be molded

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:38.770
into all kinds of things

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:42.330
from straws to containers to coat hangers.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:44.830
Then when you're done with
the objects, you ditch them,

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:47.910
and they gracefully turn
back into plant matter.

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:51.733
We make our materials out
of wood and natural binders.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:56.580
And these plant-based
binders, they degrade,

00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:00.453
so they can be eaten by naturally
occurring microorganisms.

00:13:00.453 --> 00:13:02.623
Okay.
So no microplastic left.

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.640
Sulapac has pulled in
some big-name investors,

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:10.763
including Chanel,

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:14.320
and has started making
its products in factories

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:15.383
all over the world.

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:17.710
Here are their straws coming to life

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:19.363
in a factory in Saint Louis.

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:25.880
And unlike paper straws, these
wooden things actually work.

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:29.020
So with the straw, how long
would this last, compared,

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:31.410
like a paper straw, when I
put it in a drink, I mean--

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:32.560
Exactly.
It's usually melted

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:34.140
by the end of the drink.

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.290
Well, the criteria is that
if you change the drink,

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.510
you have to be able to use this one straw.

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:41.770
So the whole evening you
are able to drink mojitos,

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:43.420
for instance, which is my favorite drink,

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:45.090
with this one straw.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:46.760
We need new initiatives

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:49.123
to make these microplastic-free materials

00:13:49.123 --> 00:13:51.510
because there is so
much plastic out there.

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:53.380
With one company with one material

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:54.833
you can't solve it all.

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:05.020
Of course, if experimental protein

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.950
and plastic-induced shame
is all too much for you,

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:09.680
have no fear.

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.330
The Finns still have you covered.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:14.900
Here at a traditional Finnish restaurant,

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:16.370
a very nice set of ladies

00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:18.283
will set you up with Finnish gin,

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:24.373
scrumptious reindeer mousse,
a ski loaded with shots,

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:29.463
and one hell of a bear pie.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:36.510
But that's enough about
Finns and their food.

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:40.710
Next time on Hello World,

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:42.853
some good old fashioned hard tech,

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:47.153
and a visit to a sauna with a movie star.

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:50.910
It's really hard to not be honest

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:52.943
when you're so exposed and so bare.

